 "lesson_number": 7,
 "title": "Indices, Surds & Bounds",
 "practice_data": {
  "method_card": {
   "steps": [
    "For fractional indices: root first (denominator), then power (numerator)",
    "For negative indices: flip to make a fraction, then calculate the positive power",
    "For surds: find the largest square factor, simplify, rationalise if needed",
    "For bounds: find the error interval, then use upper/lower as needed"
   ],
   "title": "How to Work with Indices, Surds and Bounds",
   "content": "<p><strong>Fractional indices:</strong> \\(a^{\\frac{1}{n}} = \\sqrt[n]{a}\\) and \\(a^{\\frac{m}{n}} = (\\sqrt[n]{a})^m\\). For example, \\(8^{\\frac{2}{3}} = (\\sqrt[3]{8})^2 = 2^2 = 4\\).</p><p><strong>Negative indices:</strong> \\(a^{-n} = \\frac{1}{a^n}\\). For example, \\(5^{-2} = \\frac{1}{5^2} = \\frac{1}{25}\\).</p><p><strong>Surds:</strong> Irrational roots left in exact form: \\(\\sqrt{2}\\), \\(\\sqrt{3}\\), etc. Simplify by finding square factors: \\(\\sqrt{50} = \\sqrt{25 \\times 2} = 5\\sqrt{2}\\). To <strong>rationalise the denominator</strong> of \\(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{b}}\\), multiply top and bottom by \\(\\sqrt{b}\\): \\(\\frac{a\\sqrt{b}}{b}\\).</p><p><strong>Upper and lower bounds:</strong> When a measurement is rounded, the true value lies in a range. For a length of 5.3 cm rounded to 1 d.p.: lower bound = 5.25 cm, upper bound = 5.35 cm. The error interval is \\(5.25 \\leq x < 5.35\\). For calculations with bounds, think about which combination of upper/lower gives the maximum or minimum result.</p>",
   "example": "<p><strong>Simplify</strong> \\(\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3}}\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Multiply top and bottom by \\(\\sqrt{3}\\):</p><p>$$\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3}} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{3}$$</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Simplify: \\(\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{3} = 2\\sqrt{3}\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(2\\sqrt{3}\\)</p>"
  },
